{{!--
    WHERE A CHARACTER'S EQUIPPED GEAR WOULD GO, a row per SLOT, head to toe, with
    whatever landed nowhere after them. A gap is invisible in a list of items and
    obvious in a list of slots.

    Built on `blacksmith-list` — thumbnail, title, meta, action — which is
    exactly this row and already carries the hover, the ellipsis, the spacing and
    the empty state. It was a hand-rolled table with fixed layout, stated column
    widths and its own scrolling, all of which the API already had. The body it
    sits in scrolls too; adding another scroller produced two nested scrollbars.

    The rule this window keeps having to relearn: its chrome is Blacksmith's, and
    the way to use it is to add nothing it already does.
--}}
<div class="squire-tool-content squire-import">

    {{!-- What this screen IS, in the fewest words that prevent the two wrong
          assumptions: that it reaches into the whole inventory, and that
          whatever it decides is final. --}}
    <div class="squire-import-intro">
        <p>Taking <strong>{{actorName}}</strong>'s <strong>currently equipped</strong> gear into
           <strong>{{buildName}}</strong>. Nothing else in their inventory is touched, and nothing
           on the character changes — this only fills in the plan.</p>
        <p>Every equipped item is below with the slot it would take. Anything left
           <strong>Not mapped</strong> stays equipped on the character but is not part of this
           build — so equipping the build later would take it off. Change any of them here, or
           leave it and rearrange the figure afterwards by dragging.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="blacksmith-list" data-role="gear">{{{gearRows}}}</div>

    {{#if hasSpells}}
    <div class="squire-import-divider">
        <span>Prepared spells</span>
        <span class="squire-import-count">0 / {{limit}}</span>
    </div>
    <div class="blacksmith-list" data-role="spells">{{{spellRows}}}</div>
    {{/if}}
</div>
